%! Blank nodes and nulls.
%!
%! A blank node such as _:home is a value without a name. All mentions of
%! _:home in one file refer to the same value, and _:other would be a
%! different one. Blank nodes are local to the file they appear in: each
%! use of a module gets its own blank nodes, as does each RDF file that
%! Nemo reads.
%!
%! An existential variable such as !y also creates new values, called
%! nulls. It creates a new one for each match of the rule body, but only
%! if no existing fact already provides a value.
%!
%! The modules used here are the input files household.rls and
%! vehicles.rls (see "Input files" next to the run button).
%!
%! The example shows:
%! - how a blank node is a single value, however often a rule uses it,
%! - how each use of a module gets its own blank nodes,
%! - how blank nodes describe unnamed classes in an ontology,
%! - how an existential variable creates a value only where needed.

@use household { member = millers } as millerHouse .
@use household { member = smiths } as smithHouse .

millers(anna) .
millers(ben) .
smiths(carl) .

livesAt(?person, ?place) :- millerHouse::livesAt(?person, ?place) .
livesAt(?person, ?place) :- smithHouse::livesAt(?person, ?place) .

% The module uses _:home for every household, but each use of the module
% gets its own blank node. So Anna and Ben share a home, and Carl lives
% in a different one. With a named constant such as home instead, all
% three would live together.
housemates(?x, ?y) :- livesAt(?x, ?place), livesAt(?y, ?place), ?x != ?y .

% Blank nodes are common in ontologies. In OWL, the axiom "every bicycle
% has some wheel as a part" makes bicycle a subclass of an unnamed class:
% the things that have some wheel as a part. In RDF, this unnamed class
% is a blank node:
axiom(bicycle, subClassOf, _:r) .
axiom(_:r, onProperty, hasPart) .
axiom(_:r, someValuesFrom, wheel) .

% vehicles.rls states that every motorbike has some motor as a part, and
% also calls its unnamed class _:r. Since blank nodes are local to their
% file, the two classes stay different. With a named constant r in both
% files, every bicycle would need a motor and every motorbike a wheel.
@use vehicles as more .
axiom(?s, ?p, ?o) :- more::axiom(?s, ?p, ?o) .

type(myBike, bicycle) .
type(yourBike, bicycle) .
type(myMoped, motorbike) .
related(yourBike, hasPart, frontWheel) .
type(frontWheel, wheel) .

% Members of a class have what the class requires. The existential
% variable !y creates a wheel for myBike and a motor for myMoped. The
% wheel of yourBike is already known, so no new value is created for it:
type(?x, ?d) :- type(?x, ?c), axiom(?c, subClassOf, ?d) .
related(?x, ?p, !y), type(!y, ?c) :-
    type(?x, ?r), axiom(?r, onProperty, ?p), axiom(?r, someValuesFrom, ?c) .
parts(?thing, ?part, ?class) :- related(?thing, hasPart, ?part), type(?part, ?class) .

@output livesAt, housemates, parts .
